          "claim": "By [F1], choose a connected compact smooth oriented Seifert surface $\\Sigma\\subset S^3$ with $\\partial\\Sigma=K$. By [F3], choose $0<\\varepsilon<1$ and identify a collar of $S^3=\\partial B^4$ with $S^3\\times[0,\\varepsilon)$, where $(x,u)$ maps to $(1-u)x$. Apply the collar theorem in [F3] also to $\\Sigma$ and write $r\\ge0$ for its inward boundary-collar coordinate. Choose a smooth cutoff $\\chi(r)$ equal to $1$ near $r=0$ and $0$ before the collar ends, and a constant $c\\in(0,\\varepsilon)$; set $h=\\chi(r)r+(1-\\chi(r))c$ in the collar and $h=c$ beyond it. Since $0\\le r,c<\\varepsilon<1$, $h$ takes values in $[0,1)$; it is smooth, vanishes exactly on $\\partial\\Sigma$, and is positive on the interior.",
